  "live_records": {
    "q-38686": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38686",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ 5x-3y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{18}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
