    "q-80666": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80666",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x-14y \\\\ 3x+21y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot 7-14\\cdot k \\\\ 3\\cdot 7+21\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-14-14k \\\\ 21+21k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14-14k=0 \\\\ 21+21k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
