    "q-81393": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81393",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}9y-3x+6z \\\\ 3y-x+2z \\\\ 6x-12z-18y\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -18 & -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -18 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -18 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $2$ non-pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd ones). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
