    "q-140051": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140051",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x-2y-2z \\\\ -z \\\\ x+2y+3z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$",
      "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-(-6)-2\\cdot k-2\\cdot 0 \\\\ -0 \\\\ -6+2\\cdot k+3\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6-2k \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6+2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6-2k=0 \\\\ -6+2k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to these two equations is $k=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
